  "project.chess-game.title": "3D Chess",
  "project.chess-game.summary": "A full-featured chess game with Three.js rendering and real-time multiplayer",
  "project.chess-game.description": "After working with WebGPU and understanding the internals of a 3D engine, I wanted to try a more concrete use of browser 3D with Three.js. Chess was the obvious choice: complex rules, 3D rendering, real-time multiplayer. Each piece is modelled in Blender and dynamically lit. The rules engine handles legal moves, en passant, castling and promotions. Multiplayer is in development and will run over WebSockets. Full 3D piece rendering comes right after: I've already modelled all the base pieces and the board in Blender.",

  "project.webgpu-cube.title": "WebGPU Engine",
  "project.webgpu-cube.summary": "A voxel rendering engine from scratch using raw WebGPU: no 3D library",
  "project.webgpu-cube.description": "Launched 1-2 months after WebGPU landed in stable Chrome, this project started with a simple curiosity: understand low-level graphics using my existing JS skills, without going through Three.js or Babylon. No 3D library: GPU pipelines, buffers and WGSL shaders written by hand. I reached advanced rendering with textures and the basics of a voxel engine. The real obstacles: 3D maths, engine architecture, and the inherent limitations of JS/GPU. Goal achieved: understanding what's actually happening under the hood.",

  "project.slay-the-slime.title": "Slay the Slime",
  "project.slay-the-slime.summary": "A simple clicker rendered in pure HTML/CSS: no canvas",
  "project.slay-the-slime.description": "A self-imposed absurd constraint: build a game using only HTML and CSS for rendering: no canvas, no animated SVG, no game library. This project started from a whim and turned into a genuine love story with Vue.js. Once the goal was reached proving it was possible, I stopped. The challenge was there, it was met.",

  "project.db-dofus-retro.title": "DB Dofus Retro",
  "project.db-dofus-retro.summary": "A crafting and inventory management tool for Dofus Retro",
  "project.db-dofus-retro.description": "A player's need: I needed a tool to track my crafts and the resources required. Out of pure enjoyment, I decided to build it myself so I could tailor it to my needs and have some fun along the way. This project also served as a way to keep up with new tools: and to learn how to use Claude, which was becoming essential in my work.",

  "project.tactical-mmo.title": "2D Tactical MMO",
  "project.tactical-mmo.summary": "A 2D isometric tactical MMO in C#: the dream project",
  "project.tactical-mmo.description": "This is the childhood dream project: I've always wanted to make a video game. I chose Unity because I've already had the chance to use it and because it has the most tutorials, but also to develop in C#, a language I particularly enjoy. The 2D isometric view and turn-based tactical combat are very clearly inspired by the game Dofus, which is a childhood favourite of mine, as well as for the technical interest it represents. I intend to implement multiplayer: every aspect raises deep and fascinating problems. It's still very early stage and I'll often take breaks, but this is the one I most want to finish.",

  "project.vue-translate.title": "Vue Translate",
  "project.vue-translate.summary": "A reactive, type-safe i18n plugin for Vue 3: published on npm",
  "project.vue-translate.description": "Born from three simultaneous reasons: I needed it, I wanted to build it myself, and I wanted to prove it could be done with zero heavy dependencies. v-translate directive, async JSON file loading, native Vue 3 reactivity, Zod validation, SelectLanguage component with flags. Lightweight, customisable, opinionated. This very website runs on it in production.",

  "project.observable.title": "Observable",
  "project.observable.summary": "A zero-dependency TypeScript Observable library: published on npm",
  "project.observable.description": "vue-translate needed a lightweight reactive layer. Rather than pulling in RxJS, I implemented the Observable/Observer pattern from scratch. Simple API: create reactive sources, subscribe, transform. Zero dependencies. Published on npm because if it's useful to me, it might be useful to others.",

  "project.web-api-boilerplate.title": "Web API Boilerplate",
  "project.web-api-boilerplate.summary": "A ready-to-run TypeScript/Fastify/Hono API template",
  "project.web-api-boilerplate.description": "After starting enough API projects, I eventually standardised my approach. This boilerplate is my opinionated base: TypeScript, first on Fastify then on Hono, covering project structure and recurring utilities. Instead of reconfiguring the same things for every new project, I clone it and start coding what actually matters.",

  "project.in-stock.title": "In Stock",
  "project.in-stock.summary": "An e-commerce stock availability monitor",
  "project.in-stock.description": "In 2021, a friend threw me a challenge during the great component shortage: monitor a GPU's availability and alert me the moment it comes back in stock. Result: a Node.js/TypeScript app that scrapes e-commerce sites and sends real-time alerts. Fun, useful, and I learned a lot about scraping and notification systems.",

  "project.minecraft-rust.title": "Minecraft in Rust",
  "project.minecraft-rust.summary": "A Minecraft-inspired 3D voxel engine: a Rust learning project",
  "project.minecraft-rust.description": "My favourite approach to learning a new language: build something concrete and visually satisfying. To learn Rust, I chose a Minecraft-style voxel engine: memory management without a garbage collector, low-level performance, procedural terrain generation. Rust is demanding, but the guarantees it offers in return are fascinating.",

  "project.directx-engine.title": "DirectX Engine",
  "project.directx-engine.summary": "A basic 3D rendering engine with DirectX and C++",
  "project.directx-engine.description": "Same logic as with Rust and Minecraft: learn C++ and DirectX through practice. The goal was to understand what Unity and Unreal do under the hood: graphics pipeline, vertex buffers, HLSL shaders, Win32 window management. Once the fundamentals were understood, my curiosity pulled me elsewhere. But now I know exactly what happens when a game engine draws a pixel.",

  "project.super-bingo.title": "Super Bingo",
  "project.super-bingo.summary": "A full-stack multiplayer bingo with custom word grids",
  "project.super-bingo.description": "Sometimes a project doesn't need a grand justification: the idea was fun, so I started it. Multiplayer bingo with fully customisable word grids and a community system. Full-stack architecture: Fastify/TypeScript API + Vue 3 interface. In development: game rooms, host role, visual effects."
